WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.120
Big, fire-breathing dragons. They’re just
beasts from fairy tales and fantasy fiction,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:12.059
right? That’s basically true, but those
stories only tell a fraction of the dragon’s

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.540
vast history. And I’m here today to tell
you that dragons are real…Sort of.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:33.000
We like dragons because let’s be honest,
they look cool. Many of us have pets, and

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:38.720
the dragon is the ultimate fantasy—something
beautiful yet ferocious, both loyal and capable

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.480
of protecting us.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.300
Dragons have been a part of human culture
for thousands of years.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.940
Countless countries and cultures have integrated
stories of large serpentine creatures into

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.050
their oral storytelling, art, and literature—but
why?

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.790
Early discoveries of inexplicable natural
phenomenon let our imagination run wild.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:05.640
The mystifying nature of the dragon can most
probably be accounted for by human encounters

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.810
with an astounding creature; like a large
aquatic animal floating just below the surface.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:16.150
An abnormally large snake, even an aggressive
Komodo dragon became legendary monsters.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:22.479
In the 13th Century, Marco Polo famously claimed
to encounter “huge serpents” with two

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:28.180
legs and sharp teeth while traveling in China,
writing “The jaws are wide enough to swallow

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:34.350
a man. The teeth are large and sharp and their
whole appearance is so formidable, that neither

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.840
man, nor any kind of animal can approach them
without terror.”

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:45.329
Now, it is generally believed that these “dragons”
Polo spoke of were in fact... just crocodiles.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:49.630
Dragon myths are in part a response to these
encounters and there is a rich history to

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:54.799
them that spans across many cultures. Each
dragon is unique to the time and place of

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:55.970
its creation.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:02:00.829
This is why there is no one “type” of
dragon. Not all breathe fire, some don’t

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:03.329
even have wings! Or feet!

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:08.940
This is a dragon. But so is this. Even these
guys are all considered dragons.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.820
But what accounts for such wide differences
in appearance?

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.190
While there are examples of dragons all across
the globe, from the benevolent, shape shifting

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:22.580
dragons of China to the feathered Mesoamerican
dragon-god, for now let’s just focus on

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:28.450
those most common in fantasy literature — the
Classical dragon and one of my personal favorites,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.720
the Wyvern.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.310
The wyvern is a dragon frequently associated
with the United Kingdom. It’s distinct from

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:40.030
other types of dragons because its reptilian
body possesses wings but only two legs.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.010
George R. R. Martin adopted the wyvern-style
of dragon. Khaleesi's reptilian children have

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:50.200
very long necks and tails, large wings, and
only hind legs.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.200
Martin considered biology alongside myth when
crafting Drogon and his siblings, arguing

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:02.380
that living or dead, from bat to pterodactyl,
“No beast in nature has four legs AND wings.”

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:07.900
Well, tell that to the creators of the “Classical
dragon.” Recognizable by their four legs

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.970
and powerful wings, this form of dragon is
the one most commonly seen today.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.840
So how do we explain these differences?

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.960
Before paleontology existed, skeletal remains
were not always recognizable as the bones

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.040
belonging to long dead animals.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.170
The prehistoric marine reptiles Plesiosaurus
were large, long-necked creatures with four

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:33.640
long flippers. Plesiosaurus fossils easily
could have been mistaken for the two wings

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.650
and two legs of a wyvern, or even the four
legs of a classical dragon.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:44.430
Even today we find more evidence that previous
cultures encountered reptilian fossils. In

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:50.610
2015, an ancient fossil was discovered that
appeared serpentine and has four legs.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:56.530
And a recent discovery in China revealed this
two-legged dinosaur with a pair of bat-like

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:57.530
wings.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.410
I told you dragons were real!

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:05.820
J.R.R. Tolkien had his own taxonomic system
for dragons. He classifies the creatures by

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:11.150
their capacity to breathe fire and how they
move around. His writings depict dragons with

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:16.870
and without wings, with legs and without,
and only some capable of breathing fire.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:22.139
His most famous dragon, the clever, treasure-guarding
“wicked worm” Smaug, checks off multiple

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:28.539
boxes in Tolkien’s taxonomy. Like many authors
today, Tolkien combines different dragon myths

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:31.389
to create the most terrifying beast imaginable.

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:36.210
The most iconic trait of the dragon its ability
to breath fire, but where did this attribute

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.340
come from?

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:40.939
The development of fire-breathing dragons
could have been a result of the geological

00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:46.110
features of Northern Europe, specifically
the presence of natural gas vents.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:50.810
A wayward lightning strike or dropped torch
by a miner could have provided the spark that

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:55.960
sent a tongue of flame or wave of noxious
gas to the surface. Not having a way to explain

00:04:55.960 --> 00:05:00.840
this natural phenomenon, we filled in the
gaps with a supernatural explanation—the

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.610
fire-breathing dragon.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:07.129
This may also partially explain why dragons
are associated with guarding treasure, often

00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:11.980
below ground or in dark caves. Miners would
have been likely trying to deter thieves with

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.830
scary stories of a monstrous creature protecting
their own fortunes.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.180
Depictions of the dragon as a fire-breathing
creature appear as early as the 10th century,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.270
when the gates of hell were said to be the
flaming mouth of a demonic beast.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:30.889
So what can we make from thousands of years
of stories and images of dragons? What can

00:05:30.889 --> 00:05:33.099
these tales teach us?

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:38.550
The dragon represents a human appreciation
and respect for the power of nature.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:43.780
They remind us to tread carefully across rivers,
to avoid dark caverns, and to humble us in

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.440
the presence of beasts stronger than ourselves.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.749
The dragon is legendary—it doesn’t really
matter so much whether or not it existed.

00:05:52.749 --> 00:05:57.421
What does matter is that in an age when nature
was mostly unexplained, dragons were a way

00:05:57.421 --> 00:06:02.000
to make sense of a world that seemed much
more mysterious.

